        ("h3", r"Inputs $x_i$: what the neuron sees"),
        ("p", r"Inputs are just numbers. A neuron knows nothing about flats, images or words: all of these are first turned into numbers. For a flat, $x_1 = 60$ (floor area, m²) and $x_2 = 2$ (rooms); for a 28×28 black-and-white image, $n = 784$ pixel brightnesses; for a neuron deep inside a network, the inputs are the outputs of the neurons in the previous layer."),
        ("p", r"Inputs are not learned: they are data and come from outside. But their scale matters. If $x_1$ is income in Uzbek soums (hundreds of thousands) and $x_2$ is the number of children (single digits), the first input will overwhelm the second even before training. That is why features are usually standardised: subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation."),

        ("h3", r"Weights $w_i$: what matters to the neuron"),
